and `getProduct` returns an object with `id` field. The `productId` parameter in `getProduct` maps to the product's `id` field.\n\nFor productId generation, I should use `uuid`. The package.json lists `uuid` as a dependency.\n\nLet me now implement all services. I'll create the files.\n\nLet me also think about edge cases:\n1. Catalog: what if updateProduct is called for a non-existent product? Should it silently fail? I'll make it a no-op if not found.\n2. Inventory: getStock for unknown product returns 0. Release for unknown product - just add to stock (which would set it to quantity since default is 0).\n3. Cart: addItem for non-existent cart? Probably should throw or silently no-op. I'll validate cart exists.\n4. Orders: getOrder for non-existent returns null. updateStatus for non-existent should be a no-op.\n5. Payment: processPayment for non-existent order? Probably should still work, just processing payment for an orderId.\n\nLet me write all the services now.\n\nActually, first let me check the `uuid` import style. Since it's module: \"Node16\", I need to check if uuid is ESM-compatible or if I need to use default import.\n\nuuid v10 should support ESM imports. I'll use:\n```typescript\nimport { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';\n```\n\nNow let me write all the services:\n\n### src/catalog/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus, Events, ProductCreatedEvent, CatalogService } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createCatalogService(bus: EventBus): CatalogService {\n  const products = new Map<string, { id: string; name: string; price: number; category: string }>();\n\n  return {\n    createProduct(name: string, price: number, category: string): string {\n      const id = uuidv4();\n      products.set(id, { id, name, price, category });\n      const payload: ProductCreatedEvent = { productId: id, name, price, category };\n      bus.emit(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, payload);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getProduct(productId: string) {\n      return products.get(productId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    listProducts(category?: string) {\n      const all = Array.from(products.values());\n      if (category) {\n        return all.filter(p => p.category === category);\n      }\n      return all;\n    },\n\n    searchProducts(query: string) {\n      const lower = query.toLowerCase();\n      return Array.from(products.values()).filter(\n        p => p.name.toLowerCase().includes(lower) || p.category.toLowerCase().includes(lower)\n      );\n    },\n\n    updateProduct(productId: string, updates: Partial<{ name: string; price: number; category: string }>) {\n      const product = products.get(productId);\n      if (product) {\n        if (updates.name !== undefined) product.name = updates.name;\n        if (updates.price !== undefined) product.price = updates.price;\n        if (updates.category !== undefined) product.category = updates.category;\n      }\n    },\n\n    removeProduct(productId: string): boolean {\n      return products.delete(productId);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to check: can bus.emit be called without await? Since we're not returning a promise from createProduct (it returns string, not Promise<string>), we can't await bus.emit. But bus.emit returns a Promise. The issue is that if we don't await it, the handlers might not have run by the time createProduct returns. But the interface doesn't require the handlers to have run - it just says \"Emit events when products are created.\"\n\nActually, looking more carefully, the emit method is async because it awaits each handler. But the service methods are synchronous. So we fire-and-forget the emit. This is fine - the emit will complete eventually.\n\nBut wait, there might be an issue if test code relies on events being emitted synchronously. Let me look at the types again...\n\nThe EventBus.emit is async:\n```typescript\nasync emit(event: string, payload: unknown): Promise<void> {\n    this.eventLog.push({ event, payload, timestamp: Date.now() });\n    const handlers = this.handlers.get(event);\n    if (handlers) {\n      for (const handler of handlers) {\n        await handler(payload);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n```\n\nAnd the service methods are synchronous. So we fire-and-forget. That's fine for most use cases. The events will be emitted, just not synchronously.\n\nHmm, but for the analytics service to track things, the events need to be emitted. And for tests that create a product and then check analytics, they might need to wait for the event loop. But that's the nature of async events - you'd need to await something.\n\nOK, I think this is fine. Let me proceed.\n\nActually, wait. Let me look at how the handler works when it's async. The emit calls `await handler(payload)`. If handlers are async, they need to be awaited for emit to complete. But if we fire-and-forget, the handlers will still eventually execute because the promise is doing work in the microtask queue.\n\nLet me proceed with the implementation.\n\n### src/inventory/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus, Events, InventoryUpdatedEvent, LowStockAlertEvent, InventoryService } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createInventoryService(bus: EventBus): InventoryService {\n  const stock = new Map<string, number>();\n  const thresholds = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  function checkLowStock(productId: string) {\n    const current = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    const threshold = thresholds.get(productId);\n    if (threshold !== undefined && current <= threshold) {\n      const payload: LowStockAlertEvent = { productId, currentQuantity: current, threshold };\n      bus.emit(Events.LOW_STOCK_ALERT, payload);\n    }\n  }\n\n  return {\n    setStock(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      stock.set(productId, quantity);\n      const payload: InventoryUpdatedEvent = { productId, quantity };\n      bus.emit(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, payload);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n\n    getStock(productId: string): number {\n      return stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    },\n\n    reserve(productId: string, quantity: number): boolean {\n      const current = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      if (current < quantity) {\n        return false;\n      }\n      stock.set(productId, current - quantity);\n      const payload: InventoryUpdatedEvent = { productId, quantity: current - quantity };\n      bus.emit(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, payload);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n      return true;\n    },\n\n    release(productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const current = stock.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      stock.set(productId, current + quantity);\n      const payload: InventoryUpdatedEvent = { productId, quantity: current + quantity };\n      bus.emit(Events.INVENTORY_UPDATED, payload);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n\n    setLowStockThreshold(productId: string, threshold: number): void {\n      thresholds.set(productId, threshold);\n      checkLowStock(productId);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### src/cart/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus, Events, CartUpdatedEvent, CartService } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createCartService(bus: EventBus): CartService {\n  const carts = new Map<string, { id: string; userId: string; items: Map<string, number> }>();\n\n  function emitCartUpdate(cartId: string) {\n    const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n    if (!cart) return;\n    const items = Array.from(cart.items.entries()).map(([productId, quantity]) => ({ productId, quantity }));\n    const payload: CartUpdatedEvent = { cartId, userId: cart.userId, items };\n    bus.emit(Events.CART_UPDATED, payload);\n  }\n\n  return {\n    createCart(userId: string): string {\n      const id = uuidv4();\n      carts.set(id, { id, userId, items: new Map() });\n      emitCartUpdate(id);\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    addItem(cartId: string, productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      const existing = cart.items.get(productId) ?? 0;\n      cart.items.set(productId, existing + quantity);\n      emitCartUpdate(cartId);\n    },\n\n    removeItem(cartId: string, productId: string): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      cart.items.delete(productId);\n      emitCartUpdate(cartId);\n    },\n\n    getCart(cartId: string) {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return null;\n      const items = Array.from(cart.items.entries()).map(([productId, quantity]) => ({ productId, quantity }));\n      return { id: cart.id, userId: cart.userId, items };\n    },\n\n    clearCart(cartId: string): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      cart.items.clear();\n      emitCartUpdate(cartId);\n    },\n\n    updateQuantity(cartId: string, productId: string, quantity: number): void {\n      const cart = carts.get(cartId);\n      if (!cart) return;\n      if (quantity <= 0) {\n        cart.items.delete(productId);\n      } else {\n        cart.items.set(productId, quantity);\n      }\n      emitCartUpdate(cartId);\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### src/pricing/index.ts\n\nI need to track prices from ProductCreatedEvent and store discount codes.\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus, Events, ProductCreatedEvent, PriceCalculatedEvent, PricingService } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createPricingService(bus: EventBus): PricingService {\n  const prices = new Map<string, number>();\n  const discounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  bus.on(Events.PRODUCT_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as ProductCreatedEvent;\n    prices.set(event.productId, event.price);\n  });\n\n  return {\n    calculateTotal(\n      items: Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number; price: number }>,\n      discountCode?: string\n    ): { subtotal: number; discount: number; tax: number; total: number } {\n      const subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.quantity * item.price, 0);\n      \n      let discount = 0;\n      if (discountCode && discounts.has(discountCode)) {\n        const percentage = discounts.get(discountCode)!;\n        discount = Math.round(subtotal * percentage) / 100;\n      }\n\n      const discountedSubtotal = subtotal - discount;\n      const tax = Math.round(discountedSubtotal * 0.1 * 100) / 100;\n      const total = Math.round((discountedSubtotal + tax) * 100) / 100;\n\n      return { subtotal, discount, tax, total };\n    },\n\n    addDiscountCode(code: string, percentage: number): void {\n      discounts.set(code, percentage);\n    },\n\n    getPrice(productId: string): number {\n      return prices.get(productId) ?? 0;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nWait, should I emit `PRICE_CALCULATED`? The spec says the pricing service should \"Calculate order totals including tax\" and there's an event for it. Let me check... the spec for Pricing service doesn't explicitly say to emit events, but there's a PRICE_CALCULATED event constant and a PriceCalculatedEvent type. The \"Architecture\" section says \"All inter-service communication happens through events on the bus.\" So emitting from calculateTotal makes sense, especially since there's a specific event type defined.\n\nLet me add the emit.\n\n### src/orders/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus, Events, OrderCreatedEvent, OrderStatusChangedEvent, OrderService, OrderStatus } from '../types.js';\n\nconst VALID_TRANSITIONS: Record<OrderStatus, OrderStatus[]> = {\n  [OrderStatus.PENDING]: [OrderStatus.CONFIRMED, OrderStatus.CANCELLED],\n  [OrderStatus.CONFIRMED]: [OrderStatus.SHIPPED, OrderStatus.CANCELLED],\n  [OrderStatus.SHIPPED]: [OrderStatus.DELIVERED],\n  [OrderStatus.DELIVERED]: [],\n  [OrderStatus.CANCELLED]: [],\n};\n\nexport function createOrderService(bus: EventBus): OrderService {\n  const orders = new Map<string, { id: string; userId: string; status: OrderStatus; total: number }>();\n  const userOrders = new Map<string, string[]>(); // userId -> orderIds\n\n  return {\n    createOrder(userId: string, cartId: string, total: number): string {\n      const id = uuidv4();\n      orders.set(id, { id, userId, status: OrderStatus.PENDING, total });\n\n      const userList = userOrders.get(userId) ?? [];\n      userList.push(id);\n      userOrders.set(userId, userList);\n\n      const payload: OrderCreatedEvent = { orderId: id, userId, cartId, total };\n      bus.emit(Events.ORDER_CREATED, payload);\n\n      return id;\n    },\n\n    getOrder(orderId: string) {\n      return orders.get(orderId) ?? null;\n    },\n\n    updateStatus(orderId: string, status: OrderStatus): void {\n      const order = orders.get(orderId);\n      if (!order) return;\n\n      const allowed = VALID_TRANSITIONS[order.status];\n      if (!allowed.includes(status)) return;\n\n      const previousStatus = order.status;\n      order.status = status;\n\n      const payload: OrderStatusChangedEvent = { orderId, status, previousStatus };\n      bus.emit(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, payload);\n    },\n\n    listOrders(userId: string) {\n      const orderIds = userOrders.get(userId) ?? [];\n      return orderIds\n        .map(id => orders.get(id)!)\n        .filter(Boolean)\n        .map(({ id, status, total }) => ({ id, status, total }));\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### src/payment/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { v4 as uuidv4 } from 'uuid';\nimport { EventBus, Events, PaymentProcessedEvent, PaymentService, PaymentStatus } from '../types.js';\n\nexport interface PaymentOptions {\n  simulateSuccess?: boolean;\n  simulateFailure?: boolean;\n  simulateTimeout?: boolean;\n}\n\nexport function createPaymentService(bus: EventBus, options?: PaymentOptions): PaymentService {\n  const transactions = new Map<string, { orderId: string; amount: number; status: PaymentStatus }>();\n\n  const shouldSucceed = options?.simulateSuccess ?? true;\n  const shouldFail = options?.simulateFailure ?? false;\n  const shouldTimeout = options?.simulateTimeout ?? false;\n\n  return {\n    async processPayment(orderId: string, amount: number) {\n      // Simulate async processing\n      await new Promise(resolve => setTimeout(resolve, 5));\n\n      let status: PaymentStatus;\n      let transactionId: string | undefined;\n      let error: string | undefined;\n\n      if (shouldTimeout) {\n        status = PaymentStatus.TIMEOUT;\n        error = 'Payment timed out';\n      } else if (shouldFail) {\n        status = PaymentStatus.FAILED;\n        error = 'Payment failed';\n      } else {\n        status = PaymentStatus.SUCCESS;\n        transactionId = uuidv4();\n      }\n\n      if (transactionId) {\n        transactions.set(transactionId, { orderId, amount, status });\n      }\n\n      const payload: PaymentProcessedEvent = { orderId, status, transactionId, error };\n      bus.emit(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, payload);\n\n      return { status, transactionId, error };\n    },\n\n    async refund(transactionId: string): Promise<boolean> {\n      const tx = transactions.get(transactionId);\n      if (!tx || tx.status !== PaymentStatus.SUCCESS) {\n        return false;\n      }\n      tx.status = PaymentStatus.FAILED; // mark as refunded\n      return true;\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, the refund logic. If I mark the transaction as FAILED, that might be confusing. Let me think about what makes sense. The PaymentProcessedEvent doesn't have a \"refunded\" status. But the refund method just needs to return true/false. Let me keep it simple - just check if the transaction exists and was successful, then return true.\n\nActually, looking at the spec again: \"Support refunding successful transactions. Emit events when payments are processed.\" So refund should return boolean, maybe emit a PAYMENT_PROCESSED event with a refund status? But there's no refund status in PaymentStatus. Let me just mark it internally without emitting an event for refund.\n\n### src/notifications/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus, Events, OrderCreatedEvent, OrderStatusChangedEvent, PaymentProcessedEvent, NotificationSentEvent, NotificationService } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createNotificationService(bus: EventBus): NotificationService {\n  const subscribers = new Set<string>();\n  const notifications = new Map<string, Array<{ type: string; message: string; timestamp: number }>>();\n  const orderOwners = new Map<string, string>(); // orderId -> userId\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n    orderOwners.set(event.orderId, event.userId);\n    addNotification(event.userId, 'order_created', `Order ${event.orderId} created for $${event.total}`);\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_STATUS_CHANGED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderStatusChangedEvent;\n    const userId = orderOwners.get(event.orderId);\n    if (userId) {\n      addNotification(userId, 'order_status', `Order ${event.orderId} changed from ${event.previousStatus} to ${event.status}`);\n    }\n  });\n\n  bus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n    const userId = orderOwners.get(event.orderId);\n    if (userId) {\n      addNotification(userId, 'payment', `Payment for order ${event.orderId}: ${event.status}`);\n    }\n  });\n\n  function addNotification(userId: string, type: string, message: string) {\n    if (!subscribers.has(userId)) return;\n    const userNotifications = notifications.get(userId) ?? [];\n    const timestamp = Date.now();\n    userNotifications.push({ type, message, timestamp });\n    notifications.set(userId, userNotifications);\n    const payload: NotificationSentEvent = { userId, type, message };\n    bus.emit(Events.NOTIFICATION_SENT, payload);\n  }\n\n  return {\n    subscribe(userId: string): void {\n      subscribers.add(userId);\n    },\n\n    getNotifications(userId: string) {\n      return notifications.get(userId) ?? [];\n    },\n  };\n}\n```\n\n### src/analytics/index.ts\n\n```typescript\nimport { EventBus, Events, CartUpdatedEvent, OrderCreatedEvent, PaymentProcessedEvent, AnalyticsService } from '../types.js';\n\nexport function createAnalyticsService(bus: EventBus): AnalyticsService {\n  const eventCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n  let revenue = 0;\n  const seenCarts = new Set<string>();\n  let orderCount = 0;\n  const cartItems = new Map<string, Array<{ productId: string; quantity: number }>>();\n  const productOrderCounts = new Map<string, number>();\n\n  // Listen to all events for counting\n  const allEvents = Object.values(Events);\n  for (const event of allEvents) {\n    bus.on(event, (_payload: unknown) => {\n      eventCounts.set(event, (eventCounts.get(event) ?? 0) + 1);\n    });\n  }\n\n  // Track cart contents\n  bus.on(Events.CART_UPDATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as CartUpdatedEvent;\n    cartItems.set(event.cartId, event.items);\n    seenCarts.add(event.cartId);\n  });\n\n  // Track orders and product quantities\n  bus.on(Events.ORDER_CREATED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as OrderCreatedEvent;\n    orderCount++;\n\n    // Track product quantities from the cart\n    const items = cartItems.get(event.cartId);\n    if (items) {\n      for (const item of items) {\n        productOrderCounts.set(item.productId, (productOrderCounts.get(item.productId) ?? 0) + item.quantity);\n      }\n    }\n  });\n\n  // Track revenue\n  bus.on(Events.PAYMENT_PROCESSED, (payload: unknown) => {\n    const event = payload as PaymentProcessedEvent;\n    if (event.status === 'success') {\n      // We don't have the amount in the event, so we can't track revenue from the event alone\n      // Actually, PaymentProcessedEvent doesn't contain amount\n      // Hmm, how do we track revenue then?\n    }\n  });\n\n  return {\n    getEventCount(eventType: string): number {\n      return eventCounts.get(eventType) ?? 0;\n    },\n\n    ge